# Cursor Rules

This project follows strict team development standards. Read the skills in `.ai/skills/` before generating any code.

## Skills to load

1. `.ai/skills/architecture.md` — folder structure, class naming with mandatory type suffixes, FormRequest + DTO + Resource triad, Actions/Queries/Services layering, `final` rule, dependency direction, exception handling, model lifecycle hooks, state machines, forbidden patterns
2. `.ai/skills/testing.md` — Pest, real Postgres + RefreshDatabase, feature vs unit tests, `Http::fake()` vs interface fakes, factory patterns, what NOT to test

## Core rules

- Every concrete class is `final` unless abstract
- Class names carry their type suffix: `PlaceOrderAction`, `OrderPlacedEvent`, `CreateOrderData`, `OrderResource`, `SmsService`, `SendOrderConfirmationSmsListener`, `ImportProductsFromCsvJob`
- Folder structure: flat until 8 files of a type, then promote ALL to domain subfolders atomically. Listeners are always flat
- HTTP endpoints follow the triad: FormRequest (authorize + validate) → DTO (transport, no validation) → Action (logic) → JsonResource (output)
- DTOs use `public readonly` properties, `spatie/laravel-data` base, NO validation attributes
- Actions are synchronous, fire Events AFTER `DB::transaction()` commits
- Listeners implement `ShouldQueue` by default and do one job each
- All Services get `Service` suffix (including `OrderNumberGeneratorService`, `PdfRendererService`)
- Tests use Pest, real Postgres, `RefreshDatabase` trait
- Business logic NEVER lives in controllers, models, hooks, DTOs, or Resources
- See the forbidden-patterns list in `architecture.md` — non-negotiable

## Generation

- Always show complete files with `<?php`, `declare(strict_types=1);`, full namespace, all `use` statements
- No partial snippets unless explicitly asked
- For a new write endpoint, generate: FormRequest + DTO + Action + Resource + Controller method + route + Feature test + Action unit test

## Source-of-truth precedence

`.ai/skills/` is the source of truth, not the existing code. If existing code violates the rules, flag it; don't match its style.
